          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I stopped my blood pressure tablets two months ago and my home readings are now around 155/95. Is that an emergency, and what does guidance describe about treatment and follow-up?",
          "answer": "You should definitely seek medical advice regarding your current blood pressure readings of 155/95, which are classified as Stage 2 hypertension. Although this situation is considered routine and not an emergency, it is important to have a healthcare professional evaluate your condition, especially since you have stopped taking your blood pressure medication (source: guideline-hypertension-diagnosis-and-bp-targets).\n\nCurrent guidance suggests that treatment for high blood pressure may involve lifestyle changes or medication, depending on additional health factors. If you have other risk factors such as cardiovascular disease, diabetes, or chronic kidney issues, drug treatment alongside lifestyle changes is recommended. If these conditions are not present, lifestyle modifications may be initiated first, with a follow-up after three to six months to reassess (source: guideline-hypertension-first-line-treatment). \n\nTypically, first-line treatment options include thiazide-type diuretics, ACE inhibitors, ARBs, and dihydropyridine calcium-channel blockers (source: guideline-hypertension-first-line-treatment). After starting treatment, follow-up visits are essential, generally on a monthly basis until your target blood pressure is reached. Once you achieve your target, evaluations may be conducted every three to six months, depending on stability (source: guideline-hypertension-first-line-treatment). \n\nIn summary, it is advisable to contact your clinician to discuss resuming or adjusting your treatment plan based on your recent blood pressure readings.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
          "risk_level": "routine",
